{"api_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/triton-lang/triton/issues/10743","collected_at":"2026-09-27T21:57:10.078432+00:00","comments":[],"issue":{"active_lock_reason":null,"assignee":null,"assignees":[],"author_association":"NONE","body":"### Describe the bug\n\n### Description\n\nI ran into a correctness issue in a fused RMSNorm + partial RoPE kernel.\n\nThe kernel normalizes the full hidden row, but applies RoPE only to a suffix of the row, i.e. `rotary_dim < dim`. This pattern comes from implementing a DeepSeek V4-style partial-RoPE path, where only part of the hidden dimensions participate in rotary embedding.\n\nThe problematic store pattern is roughly:\n\n```python\n# Store the full RMSNorm output.\ntl.store(x_ptr + row_base + offsets, normed, mask=mask)\n\n# Then store the rotated RoPE suffix.\ntl.store(x_ptr + a_offsets, rotated_a, mask=pair_mask)\ntl.store(x_ptr + b_offsets, rotated_b, mask=pair_mask)\n```\n\nHere `a_offsets` and `b_offsets` are a subset of `row_base + offsets`, so the RoPE suffix is written twice:\n\n1. first by the full-row `normed` store;\n2. then by the rotated tail stores.\n\nThe source order appears to rely on an implicit \"later tail stores overwrite the earlier full-row store\" assumption. However, in practice the final values in the overlapping tail addresses can be unreliable: some tail elements can match the earlier norm-only value instead of the later rotated value.\n\nA corrected version computes the final value for each element first and stores each address only once:\n\n```python\nout = tl.where(tail_mask, rotated, normed)\ntl.store(x_ptr + row_base + offsets, out, mask=mask)\n```\n\nThis version matches the PyTorch oracle.\n\n### Question\n\nAre overlapping `tl.store` operations to the same address within a single Triton program supposed to be ordered by source order?\n\nIf source-order last-write-wins semantics are intended, this seems like a compiler/lowering/scheduling bug.\n\nIf this behavior is unsupported or unspecified, could it be documented explicitly? This pattern is easy to write accidentally in fused kernels, especially for cases like:\n\n```text\nstore full normalized row\nthen overwrite a transformed subrange\n```\n\nIt would also be useful if Triton could warn about obviously overlapping stores within the same program, when the aliasing is statically visible.\n\n### Reproducer\n\nI attached a self-contained repro script. Running it directly should reproduce the issue.\n\nThe script compares a PyTorch reference, a Triton kernel with overlapping stores, and a corrected Triton kernel that writes each output element only once. The failure is isolated to the RoPE suffix written by the overlapping stores.\n\n[overlapped_tl_store.py](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/29448381/overlapped_tl_store.py)\n\n\n\n\n### Environment details\n\nTriton version: 3.7.0\nPyTorch version: 2.12.0+cu130\nCUDA version: 13.0\nGPU: RTX 5070 Ti\nCompute capability: sm120\nOS: Ubuntu 24.04\nPython version: 3.12.13","closed_at":null,"closed_by":null,"comments":0,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/triton-lang/triton/issues/10743/comments","created_at":"2026-06-29T02:26:14Z","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/triton-lang/triton/issues/10743/events","html_url":"https://github.com/triton-lang/triton/issues/10743","id":4764542633,"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"total_blocking":0},"issue_field_values":[],"labels":[{"archived_at":null,"archived_by":null,"color":"fc2929","default":true,"description":null,"id":125679820,"name":"bug","node_id":"MDU6TGFiZWwxMjU2Nzk4MjA=","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/triton-lang/triton/labels/bug"}],"labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/triton-lang/triton/issues/10743/labels{/name}","locked":false,"milestone":null,"node_id":"I_kwDOAWaHXs8AAAABG_0mqQ","number":10743,"performed_via_github_app":null,"pinned_comment":null,"reactions":{"+1":0,"-1":0,"confused":0,"eyes":0,"heart":0,"hooray":0,"laugh